   121	  local scopes, heap contents, heap location, stack, return state, exception,
   122	  and exit code.
   123	- **A3:** The invariant fixes environment `1`, parent scope `0`, the exact
   124	  module closure, and `builtinsScope` at `-1`; its guards exclude local
   125	  `list`/`sorted` shadowing.  Fixed semantics performs all lookup, argument
   126	  evaluation, allocation, method dispatch, return, and frame restoration.
   127	- **A4:** Summary cases are exhaustive and disjoint, recursion descends, map
   128	  equations are standard finite-map equalities on their full domains, and the
   129	  heap equations are total.  The guarded normalization rule applies only when
   130	  both integer keys are fresh and distinct.  No inconsistent overlap was
   131	  found.
   132	- **A5:** `CS = .IntSeq` is a realizable witness and returns the empty string.
   133	  `spec-vacuity.k` prefixes the expected result with code `0`; its `kprove`
   134	  command exits `1` with `WarnStuckClaimState` and a residual requiring
   135	  `iCons(0, RESULT) == RESULT`.
   136	
   137	## Gate B — PASS: intent adequacy
   138	
   139	- The entry domain is exactly modeled string values `str(CS)`; non-string
   140	  arguments are outside the HumanEval signature contract.  There is no hidden
   141	  restriction on length, number of words, leading/trailing spaces, or repeated
   142	  spaces.
   143	- The reference model represents characters by integer codes and the delimiter
   144	  by ASCII code `32`, matching the prompt's ASCII-order wording.  The symbolic
   145	  theorem accepts arbitrary code sequences; concrete string literals in this
   146	  semantics are ASCII-only.
   147	- The execution-to-summary connection is formally proved.  The statement that
   148	  `sortVS` is an ascending permutation is the supplied semantics' explicit
   149	  trusted primitive contract, not a theorem invented by this proof; the
   150	  human-facing conclusion is conditional on that contract and empirically
   151	  supported below.
   152	- The three prompt examples and spacing edge cases agree with the
   153	  implementation and the summary.
   154	
   155	## Gate C — PASS: trust and evidence auditability
   156	
   157	### Trust ledger
   158	
   159	| Component | Why outside this theorem | Effect and dependents | Evidence |
   160	|---|---|---|---|
   161	| Supplied `MPY` semantics and K backends | They are the required trusted language/tool foundation | Value, control, state, and proof execution; all claims | Successful LLVM/Haskell compilation, concrete traces, and `#Top` |
   162	| Fixed `sortVS` in `reference-semantics/semantics/sort.k` | Deliberately opaque under symbolic proof; its ascending-permutation contract is supplied rather than re-proved here | Returned word order and heap values; `sortedWord`, both claims | LLVM concrete implementation plus the independent finite differential suite |
   163	| Fixed `py2mpy.py` translator | Supplied, read-only front end | Identity of the Python AST proved | Regeneration of `solution.mpy`, successful parsing/execution, and visibly exact proof macros |
   164	| Proof-local derived map/freshness lemmas | K consumes them as simplifications rather than proving them as separate reachability claims | Symbolic binding and allocation reasoning in both claims; no direct control rewrite | Finite-map algebra and structural-induction derivations recorded above, totality/overlap audit, mutation probes, and concrete state traces |
   165	
   166	### Reproducible evidence
   167	
   168	- `concrete-tests.py`: six fixed tests, including all three prompt examples,
   169	  empty input, repeated/edge spaces, and descending words.  Both CPython and
   170	  LLVM/K runs pass.
   171	- `differential-tests.py`: an independent oracle that never calls `sorted`; it
   172	  orders word characters by scanning the known ASCII order `!Aa~`.  It compares
   173	  all `156` strings of lengths `0..3` over alphabet `" !Aa~"`.  CPython exits
